WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.119
A California county sees rental-assistance requests rise while sales-tax revenue weakens.

2
00:00:05.119 --> 00:00:08.110
Need is increasing just as the budget feels tighter.

3
00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:10.066
Those facts are not contradictory.

4
00:00:10.066 --> 00:00:13.402
They may be two local signs of the same economic slowdown.

5
00:00:13.402 --> 00:00:24.215
Macroeconomics provides a disciplined way to connect national output, jobs, prices, and spending to a local government's choices without pretending city hall controls the national economy.

6
00:00:24.215 --> 00:00:25.873
This is preparation only.

7
00:00:25.873 --> 00:00:30.052
No college credit or transfer credit follows from this episode.

8
00:00:30.052 --> 00:00:37.200
Gross domestic product, or GDP, is the market value of final goods and services produced within a country during a period.

9
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.016
Final prevents double counting.

10
00:00:39.016 --> 00:00:44.172
The value of flour sold to a bakery is already embodied in the bread sold to a customer.

11
00:00:44.172 --> 00:00:48.743
Production within the country counts regardless of the producer's nationality.

12
00:00:48.743 --> 00:00:56.125
Economists calculate GDP through spending as consumption plus investment plus government purchases plus exports minus imports.

13
00:00:56.125 --> 00:01:02.629
Investment includes business equipment, structures, housing construction, and inventories, not stock purchases.

14
00:01:02.629 --> 00:01:06.437
Transfer payments are not direct purchases of current production.

15
00:01:06.437 --> 00:01:09.952
Imports are subtracted because they were produced elsewhere.

16
00:01:09.952 --> 00:01:12.530
Nominal GDP values output at current prices.

17
00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:18.155
Real GDP uses constant prices to separate changes in production from changes in the price level.

18
00:01:18.155 --> 00:01:22.491
The GDP deflator is a broad price index derived from nominal and real GDP.

19
00:01:22.491 --> 00:01:30.049
Real GDP per person helps compare average material output across times or places, but it is not a complete measure of well-being.

20
00:01:30.049 --> 00:01:36.553
GDP poorly captures unpaid labor, leisure, underground activity, environmental damage, and income distribution.

21
00:01:36.553 --> 00:01:41.064
Rebuilding after disaster can raise production while people remain worse off.

22
00:01:41.064 --> 00:01:44.463
GDP remains useful, but only for the question it measures.

23
00:01:44.463 --> 00:01:48.154
Economic growth is a sustained increase in real GDP per person.

24
00:01:48.154 --> 00:01:56.474
Labor productivity, or output per hour, rises through physical capital, human capital, technology, infrastructure, and effective institutions.

25
00:01:56.474 --> 00:02:02.977
Capital faces diminishing returns when other inputs are fixed, while technology can expand productive capacity.

26
00:02:02.977 --> 00:02:07.079
Compound growth makes small annual differences important over decades.

27
00:02:07.079 --> 00:02:11.883
Growth can raise living standards while still creating pollution or unequal gains.

28
00:02:11.883 --> 00:02:16.219
The unemployment rate equals unemployed people divided by the labor force.

29
00:02:16.219 --> 00:02:22.781
To count as unemployed, a person must generally be without a job, available for work, and actively seeking work.

30
00:02:22.781 --> 00:02:26.062
The labor force includes employed and unemployed people.

31
00:02:26.062 --> 00:02:33.034
It excludes many people who are not seeking work, such as some students, retirees, caregivers, and discouraged workers.

32
00:02:33.034 --> 00:02:38.425
The labor force participation rate compares the labor force with the working-age population.

33
00:02:38.425 --> 00:02:44.577
If people stop searching, they leave the measured labor force, so unemployment can fall without new jobs.

34
00:02:44.577 --> 00:02:47.565
Frictional unemployment comes from job transitions.

35
00:02:47.565 --> 00:02:51.315
Structural unemployment reflects mismatch in skills or location.

36
00:02:51.315 --> 00:02:53.717
Cyclical unemployment rises in downturns.

37
00:02:53.717 --> 00:02:58.873
The natural rate includes frictional and structural unemployment, not zero unemployment.

38
00:02:58.873 --> 00:03:04.381
Inflation is a continuing increase in the overall price level, not merely a rise in one price.

39
00:03:04.381 --> 00:03:10.240
The consumer price index, or CPI, tracks the cost of a representative basket purchased by consumers.

40
00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:13.814
The inflation rate is the percentage change in a price index.

41
00:03:13.814 --> 00:03:17.564
Because buying patterns and quality change, no index is perfect.

42
00:03:17.564 --> 00:03:24.243
Inflation reduces purchasing power when income does not keep pace and redistributes between borrowers and lenders.

43
00:03:24.243 --> 00:03:26.294
Deflation is a falling price level.

44
00:03:26.294 --> 00:03:29.575
Disinflation means inflation remains positive but slows.

45
00:03:29.575 --> 00:03:35.785
If wages rise four percent while prices rise six percent, real purchasing power falls roughly two percent.

46
00:03:35.785 --> 00:03:40.590
The real interest rate is approximately the nominal rate minus expected inflation.

47
00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:48.382
Aggregate demand, or AD, represents planned consumption, investment, government purchases, and net exports at different price levels.

48
00:03:48.382 --> 00:03:49.906
Its curve slopes downward.

49
00:03:49.906 --> 00:03:58.519
Consumer confidence, taxes, wealth, debt, interest rates, business expectations, fiscal decisions, foreign income, and exchange rates can shift it.

50
00:03:58.519 --> 00:04:01.507
A fall in consumption or investment shifts AD left.

51
00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:07.952
Short-run aggregate supply, or SRAS, commonly slopes upward because some wages and input prices adjust slowly.

52
00:04:07.952 --> 00:04:12.463
Costs, productivity, taxes, regulations, and expected inflation can shift it.

53
00:04:12.463 --> 00:04:17.385
A cost-driven shift left can raise prices while reducing output, called stagflation.

54
00:04:17.385 --> 00:04:23.127
Long-run aggregate supply reflects capacity based on labor, capital, technology, and institutions.

55
00:04:23.127 --> 00:04:27.580
A leftward AD shift can create a recessionary gap and cyclical unemployment.

56
00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:30.158
Excessive AD can create an inflationary gap.

57
00:04:30.158 --> 00:04:36.368
The Keynesian model focuses on expenditure when prices and wages do not instantly restore full employment.

58
00:04:36.368 --> 00:04:39.825
Equilibrium occurs where planned expenditure equals output.

59
00:04:39.825 --> 00:04:46.856
Unexpected inventory growth leads firms to reduce production, while unexpected inventory loss leads them to increase it.

60
00:04:46.856 --> 00:04:51.485
The marginal propensity to consume, or MPC, is the share of added income spent.

61
00:04:51.485 --> 00:04:56.231
The marginal propensity to save, or MPS, is the share saved, and they sum to one.

62
00:04:56.231 --> 00:04:59.629
The simple multiplier is one divided by one minus the MPC.

63
00:04:59.629 --> 00:05:06.250
Taxes, imports, capacity, interest rates, expectations, and timing make real effects smaller or less predictable.

64
00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:09.414
Fiscal policy uses spending and taxes to influence AD.

65
00:05:09.414 --> 00:05:15.331
Expansionary policy addresses recession, while contractionary policy addresses inflationary pressure.

66
00:05:15.331 --> 00:05:19.140
Automatic stabilizers change with the economy without a new vote.

67
00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:23.417
Policy also faces recognition, decision, implementation, and impact lags.

68
00:05:23.417 --> 00:05:26.706
Apply the model carefully to a California local government.

69
00:05:26.706 --> 00:05:29.548
Cities and counties are not the federal government.

70
00:05:29.548 --> 00:05:36.739
They generally face balanced-budget constraints, cannot create national currency, and have limited control over aggregate demand.

71
00:05:36.739 --> 00:05:38.969
Their revenues also respond differently.

72
00:05:38.969 --> 00:05:44.598
Sales taxes can weaken quickly when households postpone vehicles, appliances, or restaurant spending.

73
00:05:44.598 --> 00:05:49.782
Property-tax revenue may move more slowly because assessment rules and billing cycles differ.

74
00:05:49.782 --> 00:05:55.579
Suppose taxable sales fall four percent, unemployment rises, and applications for housing help increase.

75
00:05:55.579 --> 00:05:57.865
First separate nominal and real measures.

76
00:05:57.865 --> 00:06:03.885
If local prices rose, the decline in real taxable activity may be greater than the nominal decline suggests.

77
00:06:03.885 --> 00:06:05.390
Second separate indicators.

78
00:06:05.390 --> 00:06:09.960
A waitlist is not GDP, and county sales-tax receipts are not national consumption.

79
00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:13.249
They are local evidence consistent with a broader downturn.

80
00:06:13.249 --> 00:06:18.377
The county should create a civic-tech dashboard with definitions, dates, and revision notes.

81
00:06:18.377 --> 00:06:29.246
Track taxable sales by broad category, benefit applications, processing time, unemployment and participation data, local job postings, CPI for the relevant region, and state or national real GDP.

82
00:06:29.246 --> 00:06:31.030
Show levels and rates of change.

83
00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:34.988
Do not mix monthly, quarterly, and annual series without labeling them.

84
00:06:34.988 --> 00:06:36.883
The dashboard should explain lags.

85
00:06:36.883 --> 00:06:42.234
Unemployment may rise after spending slows, and sales-tax remittances arrive after transactions.

86
00:06:42.234 --> 00:06:47.083
Administrative data are timely but reflect eligibility and awareness, not all hardship.

87
00:06:47.083 --> 00:06:50.651
For budgeting, build baseline, downside, and recovery scenarios.

88
00:06:50.651 --> 00:06:52.379
State the inflation assumption.

89
00:06:52.379 --> 00:06:55.110
Identify which contracts rise with a price index.

90
00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:57.785
Separate one-time reserves from ongoing revenue.

91
00:06:57.785 --> 00:07:06.035
Preserve high-value automatic responses where possible, while naming the opportunity cost of delaying a capital project or reducing another service.

92
00:07:06.035 --> 00:07:13.337
A local government can make targeted purchases or accelerate maintenance, but its action is small relative to the national economy.

93
00:07:13.337 --> 00:07:16.681
The right claim is not that one county will end a recession.

94
00:07:16.681 --> 00:07:25.154
The claim is that it can understand the cycle, protect service delivery, avoid misleading nominal comparisons, and time local choices with greater care.

95
00:07:25.154 --> 00:07:26.552
Three recall questions.

96
00:07:26.552 --> 00:07:29.529
First, why does real GDP differ from nominal GDP?

97
00:07:29.529 --> 00:07:34.695
Second, how can the unemployment rate fall without labor-market conditions improving?

98
00:07:34.695 --> 00:07:40.103
Third, what happens in the AD-AS model when aggregate demand shifts left, all else equal?

99
00:07:40.103 --> 00:07:47.603
First, nominal GDP uses current prices, while real GDP holds prices constant so changes better reflect the quantity of production.

100
00:07:47.603 --> 00:07:52.276
Second, people who stop actively looking for work leave the measured labor force.

101
00:07:52.276 --> 00:07:57.180
They are no longer counted as unemployed, so the rate can fall even without new jobs.

102
00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:08.027
Third, a leftward AD shift lowers short-run real output and the price level relative to what they otherwise would have been, creating cyclical unemployment and a possible recessionary gap.

103
00:08:08.027 --> 00:08:12.968
Artifact: Draw an AD-AS graph showing a leftward shift in aggregate demand.

104
00:08:12.968 --> 00:08:24.825
Then build a one-page county dashboard specification with six indicators, each definition, its frequency, whether it is nominal or real, and one warning against overinterpretation.

105
00:08:24.825 --> 00:08:30.886
Add a five-sentence budget scenario connecting lower taxable sales to higher service demand.

106
00:08:30.886 --> 00:08:48.211
